Identify the correspondent for this document.

A correspondent is the sender (if you received the document) or the recipient (if you sent it). It can be:
- a company or organization
- a person (use first + last name only — no titles like "Dr.", "Mr.", "Frau")
- a government agency or authority
- an email address (use ONLY if no real entity name is visible anywhere in the document)

RULES — strict, no hallucinations:

1. If the document clearly names an entity that matches one in <example_correspondents> (case- and spelling-tolerant, including minor formatting differences), output the name EXACTLY as it appears in that list.

2. Otherwise, if the document explicitly names an entity in its header, letterhead, signature, "From:" / "Absender" field, return address, or sender block, output that name as written. Preserve original spelling and language — do NOT translate names like "Bundesamt für Migration und Flüchtlinge" into {{.Language}}.

3. If no entity is clearly identifiable in the document, output exactly:
Unknown

4. Never combine multiple parties. If several entities appear (e.g. an invoice from A to B that mentions C), pick the single most relevant — usually the sender on incoming documents, the recipient on outgoing.

5. Never invent, never guess. If you are unsure between several candidates, output Unknown.

6. Legal-suffix policy: drop suffixes (GmbH, AG, Ltd, S.a.r.l., Inc., LLC) ONLY IF the <example_correspondents> list shows that convention (e.g. existing entries say "Microsoft" rather than "Microsoft Ireland Operations Limited"). Otherwise keep the name as written in the document.

7. Never output a name that appears in <blacklisted_correspondents>.

OUTPUT: exactly one line — either a correspondent name, or the literal word `Unknown`. No quotes, no commentary.

<example_correspondents>
{{.AvailableCorrespondents | join ", "}}
</example_correspondents>

<blacklisted_correspondents>
{{.BlackList | join ", "}}
</blacklisted_correspondents>

<title>{{.Title}}</title>
<content>
{{.Content}}
</content>
